# Zuca — environment variables
#
# Copy to `.env.local` for local work. Set the real values in Vercel under
# Settings → Environment Variables (Production, Preview AND Development).
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  NAMING RULE — READ THIS BEFORE ADDING A VARIABLE
#
#  Vite inlines any variable prefixed `VITE_` into the JavaScript bundle that
#  is served to every visitor. It is not a secret, it is a published constant.
#
#  NOTHING BELOW MAY EVER BE GIVEN A `VITE_` PREFIX. Doing so would recreate
#  exactly the bug this branch fixes: a write-capable webhook URL sitting in
#  the client bundle and in a public GitHub repository (SECURITY.md S3).
#
#  If you need a value in the browser, it is public. Design accordingly.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ── Google Sheets backend (required) ─────────────────────────────────────────

# The Apps Script web-app /exec URL. Server-side only — the browser now calls
# our own /api/waitlist, which calls this.
#
# This MUST be a NEW deployment. The URL currently in git history
# (commit a0c52a8) is public and write-capable; archive that deployment rather
# than reusing it. See SECURITY.md §8 item 3.
SHEETS_WEBHOOK_URL=https://script.google.com/macros/s/REPLACE_ME/exec

# Shared secret proving a write came from our server and not from anyone who
# found the URL. Must match the `ZUCA_TOKEN` script property in Apps Script.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
SHEETS_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=REPLACE_ME_WITH_64_HEX_CHARS


# ── Durable rate limiting (strongly recommended) ─────────────────────────────

# Upstash Redis, REST API. Without these two, the rate limiter falls back to a
# per-instance in-memory counter — which does NOT hold across serverless
# instances, leaving the endpoint effectively unmetered against a real flood.
#
# Free tier is sufficient: upstash.com → Create Database → Redis → REST API tab.
# These also enable server-side duplicate detection (the 409 response).
#
# CREATE THE DATABASE IN AN EU REGION (eu-west-1 / eu-central-1). Most of our
# waitlist is in Norway and the EEA. Google and Vercel are both certified under
# the EU-US Data Privacy Framework, so transfers to them are covered; Upstash is
# not on that list. Choosing an EU region removes the transfer question for this
# processor entirely rather than needing a safeguard for it. The region cannot
# be changed after creation.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=


# ── Email hashing key (strongly recommended) ─────────────────────────────────

# Pepper for the HMAC that turns an email address into a log/database handle.
#
# Without it we fall back to a plain SHA-256, and a plain hash of an email is
# NOT anonymous — the set of real addresses is small enough to enumerate with a
# wordlist. That would make the rate-limit store a second copy of the mailing
# list under GDPR. A server-held key means the hashes cannot be reversed without
# also stealing the key.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# CHANGING THIS INVALIDATES EXISTING DUPLICATE-DETECTION ENTRIES — every address
# rehashes, so previously-seen signups look new for one retention cycle. Set it
# once, before launch, and leave it alone.
EMAIL_HASH_PEPPER=REPLACE_ME_WITH_64_HEX_CHARS


# ── Bot challenge (optional, third layer — not currently wired) ──────────────

# Cloudflare Turnstile. The honeypot and timing checks are the first two layers
# and cost the user nothing; a challenge is only worth adding if logs show they
# are being beaten. Deliberately NOT enabled by default — see HANDOFF-sec.md.
#
# The site key IS public by design (it goes in the widget), so it is the one
# value here that could legitimately carry a VITE_ prefix. The secret key must
# never leave the server.
# TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=
